  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nStrong South Winds & Warm Today:\n\nAnother day of strong winds and anomalous temperatures expected\nas an upper level trough is seen strengthening on GOES upper \nlevel water vapor imagery loops and derived winds over the \nwindward side of the Rocky Mountains. As the trough phases \novernight through Friday morning, lee cyclogenesis is expected \nto quickly lift through the Dakotas into southern Canada by \ntonight. Ageostrophic descent within the tightening upper level \ngeopotential height gradient will mix and dry the mid to lower \nlevels, abating initial precipitation chances until Saturday \nevening. Therefore, strong south winds with gusts 30-35mph \nareawide this morning into Saturday morning, topping out around \n35-40mph west of the Mississippi River Valley through the late \nmorning into this evening.\n\nRainfall Saturday Evening & Night:\n\nAs the low center continues exiting well east through southern \nCanada, tightening of the low level isoheight gradient \nconcentrates prefrontal moisture transport through the Upper \nMidwest as the baroclinic boundaries amplifies before shifting \neast. The resultant IWV/IVT pushes 34mm/400 kg m-1 s-1 through \nthe warm conveyor belt with vectors meeting near 30 degrees \nalong the boundary. Therefore, ample isentropic upglide within \n1\" PWATs, record moisture for this time of year according to SPC\nRAOB climatology at KDVN & KMPX, has resulted in increased \nprecipitation probabilities within recent long term ensemble \nsolutions and high resolution models. Current confidence for \n0.5\" pushes 70% through Sunday, similar probabilities to 0.25\" \nin previous LREF member runs. REFS mean rainfall amounts near \n0.75\" in a very narrow corridor from central through northeast \nIowa into southwest Wisconsin while maximum amounts near 2\". All\nsynoptic solutions suggest zonal shifting of the baroclinic \nboundary, limiting duration of anomalous moisture and heavy \nrainfall potential.\n\nBreezy Sunday, Near to Slightly Below Normal Temperatures \nThrough Early Next Week:\n\nWhile not as strong, increased winds persist through the \nweekend, initially as the frontal boundary tightens the pressure\ngradient Saturday and subsequently from the strong low level \nCAA steepening low level lapse rates across the Upper \nMississippi River Valley. For example, the 7C 90th percentile \n(SPC climatology) 850mb temperatures Friday night quickly drop \nto near median of -1C through Saturday night, and 25th \npercentile (-6C) Sunday night. While the low level baroclinic \nboundary will linger over the Upper Mississippi River Valley \nthrough the start of the week, potentially limiting local \nimpacts from the below normal temperature potential, \nanticyclonic flow eventually shifts through the area by midweek,\nkeeping daytime highs in the 30s and overnight lows from the \nteens to 20s until then. Should low level CAA persist strong \nwinds into Sunday night, current widespread apparent \ntemperatures in the teens may decrease into the single digits in\ncoming forecasts.",
